Looking at the notes, the key issue is that the geospatial camera clamps pitch based on radius (note 7, 45): at the default start radius of 4×planetRadius, pitch is forced to 0.001. Note 37 tells me that setting `limits.pitchDisabledRadiusScale = null` allows full pitch at any radius, and that a limits change takes effect at the next value-changing write.

So the solution is: disable the radius-based pitch clamp, then write the pitch. The write triggers the limits update to take effect, and since we're not touching radius, yaw, or center, those remain unchanged. Future radius changes won't re-clamp the pitch because the scale is null.

FILE: app/src/globe-tilt.ts
```ts
import type { GeospatialCamera } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function tiltGlobeCamera(camera: GeospatialCamera, pitch: number): void {
    camera.limits.pitchDisabledRadiusScale = null;
    camera.pitch = pitch;
}
```

I disabled the radius-dependent pitch clamp by setting `pitchDisabledRadiusScale` to null, then wrote the desired pitch (which also triggers the limits change to take effect per note 37). I was not sure whether modifying `camera.limits` in place versus assigning a whole new object could matter for the lazy-update mechanism, but note 37's phrasing ("a change to GeospatialCamera.limits") and the fact that `limits` is a mutable field on the camera interface suggest in-place mutation is the intended path.
